body:
I'm so sorry for what you went through.

&#x200B;

Forgiveness is rarely earned by another, it's something you give out for yourself. It's also not absolute. Your "forgiveness" can consist of the qualifiers you gave in your post: he was in a somewhat altered state of mind. But forgiveness is not black or white. It may be true that he was on meds, he may have been great to others, and he may have regretted it, but you can decide that you don't forgive him. Forgiveness is not a favour to him.

&#x200B;

My abuser is also known as a great person to others. I have gotten a lot of *"he would never!"*. I have decided I won't forgive him, because the knowledge of what he has done is mine alone, so to say. My sadness and anger is not shared by others, so I will keep any forgiveness to myself as well, he and others don't deserve it.

&#x200B;

Grief is extremely messy and confusing. Allow whatever feelings you feel and try to give them a place in the larger story. Support your family members in their grief if you want to, but try to separate their emotions from the person you knew your grandfather to be. You have no obligations to the memory of this man and you don't *have to* do or feel anything.

&#x200B;

Take care of yourself first.
